{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs36 InCenter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Free, in-REAPER stereo re-centering for material whose stereo image is pulled to one side - portable-recorder field captures, or designed stereo assets (whooshes, blips, textures) built without watching the stereo base. See README.md for details. Requires the bundled incenter_core.lua + incenter.py and a system Python 3 with numpy.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs36 InCenter\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A free tool that re-centers a stereo image that's pulled to one side. It runs entirely inside your own REAPER session.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://budashaudio.com"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Budash Audio
}}}
 \u183? v0.9.0 \u183? MIT-licensed}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs32 Why rotation, not narrowing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The common fixes for an off-axis stereo image (mid/side width reduction, mono-summing) work by narrowing the stereo base until the offset is less noticeable. That costs you real width.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 InCenter instead measures the actual angle of the offset, per frequency band, and rotates it back to center. The stereo base keeps whatever width it had. Only its orientation changes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs32 Who it's for\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Portable-recorder captures.} Field recordings off an H1, H4, H5 or similar handheld, X/Y or A/B, where the recorder wasn't quite square to the source and the image came back pulled to one side.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Designed stereo assets.} Whooshes, blips, textures and other synthesized sound designed without ever watching the stereo base on a goniometer: the image can drift off-center in the design process just as easily as from a badly-aimed mic, and needs the same fix.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs32 A note on file formats\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 REAPER can play audio in almost any format - mp3, mp4, flac, aiff and so on - without you ever noticing the difference. InCenter is not REAPER: it reads WAV only. If an item's source is anything else, InCenter skips it, with a status line naming the format as the reason.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Watch for this specifically in a REAPER video workflow, where the same .mp4 sits on both a video track and an audio track. REAPER plays that .mp4 fine either way, so the limitation isn't obvious until you hit it: to InCenter, the audio-track copy is still an unsupported .mp4. See {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "#video-and-other-non-wav-sources"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Video and other non-WAV sources
}}}
 for the fix.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs32 Install\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Requires the {\b ReaImGui} extension (Extensions -> ReaPack -> Browse packages -> search "ReaImGui") and a system {\b Python 3} with {\f1 numpy}. If you don't already have Python set up, follow {\b {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "INSTALL_Python.md"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
INSTALL_Python.md
}}}
} first, a plain, step-by-step guide (no Python knowledge needed) for macOS, Windows and Linux. InCenter auto-detects the interpreter: it actually tries {\f1 import numpy} in each candidate, so it won't pick a Python that's missing the library.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Via ReaPack} (recommended): Extensions -> ReaPack -> Import repositories..., paste this URL, then OK:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 https://raw.githubusercontent.com/budashaudio/InCenter/main/index.xml\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Then Extensions -> ReaPack -> Synchronize packages, find InCenter under the Budash Audio category, right-click it, Install, then Apply. ReaPack then handles updates automatically.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Manual install}, as a fallback:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab Download the latest release from the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/budashaudio/InCenter/releases"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
GitHub Releases page
}}}
 and copy the whole folder into your REAPER {\f1 Scripts} folder (any subfolder is fine: each script locates itself and its siblings automatically, no fixed path is baked in).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab In REAPER: Actions -> Show action list -> New action -> Load ReaScript... and pick {\f1 BudashAudio_InCenter.lua}, inside the {\f1 Budash Audio} folder (the control panel, and the only file here you load directly).\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Do not load {\f1 incenter.py} or {\f1 incenter_core.lua} as REAPER actions.} They're dependencies: {\f1 incenter_core.lua} is the REAPER-side mechanics the panel calls into (process runner, source-swap, Python finder), and {\f1 incenter.py} is the DSP engine, meant to run only as a subprocess with CLI arguments. Loading {\f1 incenter.py} directly runs it under REAPER's own embedded Python, which can't import numpy and may hang REAPER. Both carry an {\f1 @noindex} tag so ReaPack won't list them as separate actions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs32 Use\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw365\pich822\picwgoal7300\pichgoal16440\bin }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Select one or more stereo WAV items, open the InCenter panel, and set:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Attack / Tail strength}: how hard to pull each part back to center (0-1). Tail 0 leaves the room/reverb tail where it is.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Sound length}: leave on {\b Auto} to let it pick the analysis window from the detected sound length, or force a size for unusual material.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Align}: on for spaced mics (AB); off for coincident mics (XY/MS), which have no timing offset to fix.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Stereo width}: optional, narrows the image after centering.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Width only (skip centering)}: applies stereo width reduction without re-centering, for when you only want the narrowing.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Press {\b Process selected item(s)}. Each item's take is repointed at a corrected file named {\f1 <name>_centered_<HHMMSS>.wav}, written to the project's media folder (or next to the source if the project isn't saved). The original source audio is never overwritten.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs32 What it does\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Estimates the stereo angle {\b per frequency band} (24 log-spaced bands) and rotates each band back to center. That fixes the frequency-dependent tilt a plain L/R gain trim can't touch.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Estimates it {\b separately for the attack and the tail} of each sound (onset detection on the energy envelope) and crossfades between the two corrections, since a recording's transient and its room tail often sit at different angles.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Optional {\b inter-channel delay alignment} (GCC-PHAT, sub-sample precision) for spaced-mic (AB) recordings, where part of the "wrong" image comes from a small timing offset between channels. The delay is measured on the loudest part of the region being processed, so leading silence doesn't fool it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Optional {\b stereo width reduction}, applied last and independently of centering, with RMS-matched output level.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Keeps your format and metadata.} Output bit depth and sample rate match the input (16->16, 24->24, float->float), and BWF/bext timecode, iXML and other chunks are carried across so the corrected file drops back onto the timeline exactly where the original sat.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs32 What InCenter does not do\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab It corrects a {\b static offset of the whole stereo scene}. It measures one angle per frequency band across the file and rotates each band back.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab It does {\b not} separate sources. It cannot tell a footstep from the street behind it, and it cannot move one and leave the other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab On material where sources move across the stereo base, or where the scene is already symmetric, the measured offset will be near zero and {\b no correction is applied: this is the correct result}. The status line after processing reports the measurement so you can see this for yourself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab A source moving across the image is part of the recording, and InCenter leaves it alone.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs32 Known limitations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Metadata:} standard chunks (bext, iXML, cue, LIST, junk) are carried over; exotic vendor chunks should survive too, but only the common ones are tested.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Bit depth:} 16/24/32-bit int and 32/64-bit float are supported. A 24-bit source that can't be decoded cleanly falls back to 32-bit float output.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b SECTION takes} (reversed or glued items) are skipped with a message. Glue to a plain file first if you need to process one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Item region:} processing is scoped to the item's trimmed region: an item covering the whole source file behaves as before, and a trimmed item is measured and corrected using only its own content.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Very short items:} items under about 21ms at 48kHz (1024 samples) are refused with a clear error message. Items only marginally longer than that floor still collapse to a single measured angle, since there aren't enough STFT frames for the attack/tail crossfade to mean anything. Normal attack/tail splitting resumes from roughly 22ms at 48kHz.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Windows:} the macOS and Linux paths are the tested ones. Windows support is implemented but {\b experimental}: please report back if you run it there.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs32 Video and other non-WAV sources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An item whose source is .mp4, .mp3, .flac or .aiff is skipped. If you need InCenter to correct one, render it to WAV first:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab Select the audio-copy item, not the one carrying your picture.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab Run {\b Item: Glue items}. This decodes the source into a new WAV, trimmed to the item.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 3.\tx360\tab Run InCenter on the glued item.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 InCenter replaces an item's source with the corrected WAV, so never run it on the item carrying your video: that item would lose its picture.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs32 Troubleshooting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Nothing happens / REAPER seems frozen:} first check you loaded {\f1 BudashAudio_InCenter.lua}, {\b not} {\f1 incenter.py} (the most common mistake).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b "No Python 3 found":} if you don't have a Python 3 with numpy anywhere yet, install one: see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "INSTALL_Python.md"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
INSTALL_Python.md
}}}
. If you already do (a pyenv, conda, or other custom-prefix install) but InCenter still isn't finding it, that's because auto-detection only checks the usual install locations. Set {\f1 PYTHON_OVERRIDE} near the top of {\f1 BudashAudio_InCenter.lua} to its full path instead.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b The panel freezes while processing:} this is expected. {\f1 ExecProcess} blocks the main thread until the worker exits. Batching every selected item into one process launch keeps it as short as possible, but it isn't instant on long files.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs32 Why it's built the way it is\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A few non-obvious decisions, in case you're reading the source:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b DSP runs in a subprocess.} REAPER's built-in Python can't safely import numpy (it deadlocks via ctypes/libffi inside the embedded interpreter), so all processing shells out to your system Python via {\f1 reaper.ExecProcess} with a small wrapper script that captures the real exit code to a sidecar file.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b One {\f1 incenter.py} process handles the whole batch.} {\f1 ExecProcess} blocks REAPER's UI thread until the worker exits, so N separate process launches would mean N back-to-back freezes instead of one - processing every unique source in a single {\f1 --batch} run keeps that down to one blocking call (and lets the panel wrap the whole run in a single Undo block). That's expected: the panel shows "Processing..." first so you can see it started.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Own WAV reader/writer instead of a library one.} scipy.io.wavfile (back when scipy was still a dependency) can't write 24-bit and drops metadata chunks; parsing the RIFF container directly lets input format == output format and preserves bext/iXML/cue/etc.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b The old take source is never destroyed after a swap.} Destroying a {\f1 PCM_source} still shared between takes segfaults inside {\f1 SetActiveTake}. Leaving it alone leaks a little memory per run, which is the better trade.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Peaks are rebuilt with the 3-phase {\f1 PCM_Source_BuildPeaks} protocol} after every source swap. Without it the waveform doesn't redraw.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b REAPER-side mechanics live in {\f1 incenter_core.lua}.} The process runner, the source-swap, and the Python finder are REAPER plumbing. That separation is worth keeping on its own merits, independent of how many scripts call into it.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs32 Running the tests\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 tests/test_incenter.py} covers the Python DSP engine ({\f1 pytest}); {\f1 tests/lua/incenter_core_spec.lua} covers the Lua core against a fake {\f1 reaper} API ({\f1 busted}). These are developer-only checks, separate from the runtime requirements above.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 python3 -m pip install pytest\line
python3 -m pytest tests/\line
\line
brew install lua luarocks   # macOS; use your platform's package manager\line
luarocks install busted\line
eval "$(luarocks path)"\line
busted tests/lua --lpath="tests/lua/?.lua"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs32 Requirements\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab REAPER (developed on the portable macOS build, Apple Silicon)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab ReaImGui: for the control panel only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Python 3 with {\f1 numpy}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Stereo WAV sources (24-bit/48 kHz is the primary target; other stereo WAVs work too)\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs32 Changelog\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b 0.9.0}: First public release. Per-band attack/tail centering, GCC-PHAT alignment on the loudest region, auto window selection, and item-region processing (a trimmed item is measured and corrected using only its own region of the source file, so a long recording with several events at different stereo positions - e.g. radio chatter - doesn't get averaged into one measurement). A "Width only" checkbox applies stereo width reduction without re-centering. Diagnostic reporting of what was measured after each run, format- and metadata-preserving WAV I/O, output to the project media folder. Experimental Windows support.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs32 Credits & reporting\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Built by Budash Audio. Bug reports and feedback are welcome. Please include your OS, REAPER version, and the text from the panel's status line if it showed an error.\par}
